1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,600
Fine-tuning a flow-matching robot policy with real-world reinforcement learning.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,800
Two long-horizon tasks on two robots, one method: FlowDPG.

3
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:18,400
Flow matching learns to carry noise to demonstrated actions.

4
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:24,200
Trained by imitation alone, a policy cannot recover from failures it has never seen.

5
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:29,800
Real-world RL can close this gap, but flow policies make it hard.

6
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:34,600
A flow policy makes each action chunk by integrating an ODE over many steps.

7
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:39,600
A DDPG-style update must push the critic gradient back through every step.

8
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:45,800
Memory grows with the step count, and the Jacobian product can explode or vanish.

9
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:51,600
Value-conditioning (RA-BC, AWR, RECAP) reweights demos: it only shifts mass between demonstrated modes.

10
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:57,000
Auxiliary modules (DSRL, PLD, RLT) freeze the flow and learn its noise or a residual.

11
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,400
Adjoint methods (QAM) carry the critic gradient back through every ODE step.

12
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:07,800
FlowDPG uses the critic gradient at a single point, with no backprop through the ODE.

13
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:13,200
A training sample pairs noise x₀ with a demo action x₁; xₜ lies between them.

14
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:18,600
One forward pass projects xₜ to a clean-action estimate, â.

15
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:24,200
The critic gradient at â is rescaled to the local velocity: the adaptive shift.

16
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:30,000
The shifted action a* gives a new target u* = a* − x₀, fit with one L2 loss.

17
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,800
A BC anchor keeps actions feasible. Deployment stays a plain ODE solve.

18
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:40,400
The FlowDPG gradient is vanilla DPG under three explicit approximations.

19
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:45,600
Critic at â, so ODE sensitivity is the identity; one sampled t; a normalized step.

20
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:50,800
A consistency loss keeps â near the true endpoint, where the critic is reliable.

21
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:57,000
Offline: backbone, twin-critic IQL and flow actor train together on demonstrations.

22
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,800
Online: the robot collects rollouts while critic and actor update asynchronously.

23
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:06,200
Imitation stays on the demonstrated action and fails.

24
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:14,300
FlowDPG adds a critic-driven correction toward a higher-value action a*, then distills it into the flow.

25
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:24,800
FlowDPG shows an emergent re-grasp that no demonstration contains.

26
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:33,600
The failed attempt, next to a newly explored action with higher value.
